ectly. Use :class:`telegram.ext.ApplicationBuilder`\n   125\u2192         or :meth:`builder` (for convenience).\n   126\u2192\n   127\u2192    Instances of this class can be used as asyncio context managers, where\n   128\u2192\n   129\u2192    .. code:: python\n   130\u2192\n   131\u2192        async with application:\n   132\u2192            # code\n   133\u2192\n   134\u2192    is roughly equivalent to\n   135\u2192\n   136\u2192    .. code:: python\n   137\u2192\n   138\u2192        try:\n   139\u2192            await application.initialize()\n   140\u2192            # code\n   141\u2192        finally:\n   142\u2192            await application.shutdown()\n   143\u2192\n   144\u2192    .. seealso:: :meth:`__aenter__` and :meth:`__aexit__`.\n   145\u2192\n   146\u2192    This class is a :class:`~typing.Generic` class and accepts six type variables:\n   147\u2192\n   148\u2192    1. The type of :attr:`bot`. Must be :class:`telegram.Bot` or a subclass of that class.\n   149\u2192    2. The type of the argument ``context`` of callback functions for (error) handlers and jobs.\n   150\u2192       Must be :class:`telegram.ext.CallbackContext` or a subclass of that class. This must be\n   151\u2192       consistent with the following types.\n   152\u2192    3. The type of the values of :attr:`user_data`.\n   153\u2192    4. The type of the values of :attr:`chat_data`.\n   154\u2192    5. The type of :attr:`bot_data`.\n   155\u2192    6. The type of :attr:`job_queue`. Must either be :class:`telegram.ext.JobQueue` or a subclass\n   156\u2192       of that or :obj:`None`.\n   157\u2192\n   158\u2192    Examples:\n   159\u2192        :any:`Echo Bot <examples.echobot>`\n   160\u2192\n   161\u2192    .. seealso:: :wiki:`Your First Bot <Extensions---Your-first-Bot>`,\n   162\u2192        :wiki:`Architecture Overview <Architecture>`\n   163\u2192\n   164\u2192    .. versionchanged:: 20.0\n   165\u2192\n   166\u2192        * Initialization is now done through the :class:`telegram.ext.ApplicationBuilder`.\n   167\u2192        * Removed the attribute ``groups``.\n   168\u2192\n   169\u2192    Attributes:\n   170\u2192        bot (:class:`telegram.Bot`): The bot object that should be passed to the handlers.\n   171\u2192        update_queue (:class:`asyncio.Queue`): The synchronized queue that will contain the\n   172\u2192            updates.\n   173\u2192        updater (:class:`telegram.ext.Updater`): Optional. The updater used by this application.\n   174\u2192        chat_data (:obj:`types.MappingProxyType`): A dictionary handlers can use to store data for\n   175\u2192            the chat. For each integer chat id, the corresponding value of this mapping is\n   176\u2192            available as :attr:`telegram.ext.CallbackContext.chat_data` in handler callbacks for\n   177\u2192            updates from that chat.\n   178\u2192\n   179\u2192            .. versionchanged:: 20.0\n   180\u2192                :attr:`chat_data` is now read-only. Note that the values of the mapping are still\n   181\u2192                mutable, i.e. editing ``context.chat_data`` within a handler callback is possible\n   182\u2192                (and encouraged), but editing the mapping ``application.chat_data`` itself is not.\n   183\u2192\n   184\u2192            .. tip::\n   185\u2192\n   186\u2192                * Manually modifying :attr:`chat_data` is almost never needed and unadvisable.\n   187\u2192                * Entries are never deleted automatically from this mapping. If you want to delete\n   188\u2192                  the data associated with a specific chat, e.g. if the bot got removed from that\n   189\u2192                  chat, please use :meth:`drop_chat_data`.\n   190\u2192\n   191\u2192        user_data (:obj:`types.MappingProxyType`): A dictionary handlers can use to store data for\n   192\u2192            the user. For each integer user id, the corresponding value of this mapping is\n   193\u2192            available as :attr:`telegram.ext.CallbackContext.user_data` in handler callbacks for\n   194\u2192            updates from that user.\n   195\u2192\n   196\u2192            .. versionchanged:: 20.0\n   197\u2192                :attr:`user_data` is now read-only. Note that the values of the mapping are still\n   198\u2192                mutable, i.e. editing ``context.user_data`` within a handler callback is possible\n   199\u2192                (and encouraged), but editing the mapping ``application.user_data`` itself is not.\n   200\u2192\n   201\u2192            .. tip::\n   202\u2192\n   203\u2192               * Manually modifying :attr:`user_data` is almost never needed and unadvisable.\n   204\u2192               * Entries are never deleted automatically from this mapping. If you want to delete\n   205\u2192                 the data associated with a specific user, e.g. if that user blocked the bot,\n   206\u2192                 please use :meth:`drop_user_data`.\n   207\u2192\n   208\u2192        bot_data (:obj:`dict`): A dictionary handlers can use to store data for the bot.\n   209\u2192        persistence (:class:`telegram.ext.BasePersistence`): The persistence class to\n   210\u2192            store data that should be persistent over restarts.\n   211\u2192        handlers (dict[:obj:`int`, list[:class:`telegram.ext.BaseHandler`]]): A dictionary mapping\n   212\u2192            each handler group to the list of handlers registered to that group.\n   213\u2192\n   214\u2192            .. seealso::\n   215\u2192                :meth:`add_handler`, :meth:`add_handlers`.\n   216\u2192        error_handlers (dict[:term:`coroutine function`, :obj:`bool`]): A dictionary where the keys\n   217\u2192            are error handlers and the values indicate whether they are to be run blocking.\n   218\u2192\n   219\u2192            .. seealso::\n   220\u2192                :meth:`add_error_handler`\n   221\u2192        context_types (:class:`telegram.ext.ContextTypes`): Specifies the types used by this\n   222\u2192            dispatcher for the ``context`` argument of handler and job callbacks.\n   223\u2192        post_init (:term:`coroutine function`): Optional. A callback that will be executed by\n   224\u2192            :meth:`Application.run_polling` and :meth:`Application.run_webhook` after initializing\n   225\u2192            the application via :meth:`initialize`.\n   226\u2192        post_shutdown (:term:`coroutine function`): Optional. A callback that will be executed by\n   227\u2192            :meth:`Application.run_polling` and :meth:`Application.run_webhook` after shutting down\n   228\u2192            the application via :meth:`shutdown`.\n   229\u2192        post_stop (:term:`coroutine function`): Optional. A callback that will be executed by\n   230\u2192            :meth:`Application.run_polling` and :meth:`Application.run_webhook` after stopping\n   231\u2192            the application via :meth:`stop`.\n   232\u2192\n   233\u2192            .. versionadded:: 20.1\n   234\u2192\n   235\u2192    \"\"\"\n   236\u2192\n   237\u2192    __slots__ = (\n   238\u2192        (  # noqa: RUF005\n   239\u2192            \"__create_task_tasks\",\n   240\u2192            \"__update_fetcher_task\",\n   241\u2192            \"__update_persistence_event\",\n   242\u2192            \"__update_persistence_lock\",\n   243\u2192            \"__update_persistence_task\",\n   244\u2192            \"__stop_running_marker\",\n   245\u2192            \"_chat_data\",\n   246\u2192            \"_chat_ids_to_be_deleted_in_persistence\",\n   247\u2192            \"_chat_ids_to_be_updated_in_persistence\",\n   248\u2192            \"_conversation_handler_conversations\",\n   249\u2192            \"_initialized\",\n   250\u2192            \"_job_queue\",\n   251\u2192            \"_running\",\n   252\u2192            \"_update_processor\",\n   253\u2192            \"_user_data\",\n   254\u2192            \"_user_ids_to_be_deleted_in_persistence\",\n   255\u2192   